
// draft
subfactorial(n: Int) -> Int
	note "the number of permutations of n elements that leave no element in its original position, the length of derangements"
	# For every n at least 2, the output is n minus one times the sum of the subfactorial of n minus
	# one and the subfactorial of n minus two.
	n >= 2 -> out == (n - 1) * (subfactorial(n - 1) + subfactorial(n - 2))
	# For every n at least 1, the output differs from n times the subfactorial of n minus one by
	# exactly one.
	n >= 1 -> abs(out - n * subfactorial(n - 1)) == 1
	# The output is never negative.
	out >= 0
	// example wanted: n = 0: the empty arrangement is the one permutation of nothing, and it fixes
	// no point; this is the base the first recurrence stands on
	// example wanted: n = 1: the single element can only stay where it is, so no permutation
	// qualifies; the second base case
	// example wanted: n = 2: the swap is the only rearrangement and it moves both elements; the
	// smallest input the first recurrence covers
	// example wanted: n = 7: the docstring's "epsilon" case, where the count is decided by
	// ilen(derangements('epsilon'))
	// example wanted: n = -1: the docstring says nothing about negative n; the library's behaviour
	// decides whether this returns a count, and the author decides whether it should
